WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.720
Did you ever wonder if certain foods
have a direct effect on your health?

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.640
Well, I’m here to
solve that mystery

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.160
since you are
the foods you eat.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.200
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.160
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.200
Today we answer a wide
variety of your questions,

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.240
like the best way
to treat insomnia,

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.080
or information on how
to lower your cholesterol,

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.848
and whether or not you
can eat too many beans.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.080
Hello, this is Dr. Michael Greger coming to&nbsp;
you live from my stationary treadmill because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.160
so many people complained that I was making&nbsp;
them seasick. I don't want to make people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.840
sick—that's kind of the antithesis of what&nbsp;
I'm trying to do. Let's get to your questions.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:50.960
“First one up is nutritionally, for weight&nbsp;
loss and longevity, are peanuts more like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:57.840
nuts or legumes? And which nuts have the highest&nbsp;
concentration of plant sterols to bring down LDL?”

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:05.080
Nutritionally, peanuts are very similar to tree&nbsp;
nuts. In terms of weight loss, they have a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:13.160
similar caloric density. In terms of longevity,&nbsp;
walnuts really beat all other tree nuts as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:30.400
as peanuts. And I don't think peanut butter works&nbsp;
at all. Does silica prevent AGEs? No, not that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:37.040
know of, nor would it boost collagen. I do have a&nbsp;
webinar on collagen-boosting foods; check it out.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:43.880
“Should the government tax high saturated fat&nbsp;
foods?” Whether it should or whether it could—so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:48.960
there was a... I do have videos talking about&nbsp;
the Danish meat tax, which was so effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.880
that the animal agriculture lobby made sure&nbsp;
it did not last very long, but just as we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.880 --> 00:02:00.360
starting to see significant public health&nbsp;
benefits, unfortunately, it was repealed.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:07.440
“Any tips on repopulating my gut after a course&nbsp;
of antibiotics?” Well, probiotics—taking probiotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.520
supplements actually makes things worse. It&nbsp;
actually delays the rebalancing of your gut flora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.320
which is something that I discovered in&nbsp;
researching How Not to Age. Check out my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.600
probiotics chapter. All we can do is just&nbsp;
start feeding... I mean, there are certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.920
antibiotics that absorb in the small intestine&nbsp;
and don't really affect much in the gut, but if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.840
you're experiencing gastrointestinal symptoms,&nbsp;
make sure to get lots of prebiotics to feed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:35.560
the stragglers. You know, we're not sterilizing&nbsp;
the gut; your gut microbes may just take a hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:42.880
so you feed them lots of good food, and they&nbsp;
will be fruitful and multiply on their own.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.200
“Are there any health benefits you can get from&nbsp;
coffee but not from green tea?” Absolutely. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.280
coffee has chlorogenic acid, the&nbsp;
autophagy-boosting antioxidant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.360
which green tea does not have. Three cups of&nbsp;
coffee a day decrease all-cause mortality by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.920
13%, while three cups of tea a day decrease it&nbsp;
by 24%, but by a different mechanism—actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:08.760
ironically, a pro-oxidant mechanism, rallying&nbsp;
our antioxidant defenses in an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:13.520
"that which doesn't kill us makes us&nbsp;
stronger" hormetic mechanism. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:20.440
they both work through different mechanisms, so we&nbsp;
would assume it would be an additive effect. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:26.240
eat both or drink both. I started out&nbsp;
with coffee; now I'm drinking tea.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.880
Next up, “any advice to help with sleep—not&nbsp;
just falling asleep but staying asleep?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.880
Absolutely. So, treating insomnia is all&nbsp;
about sleep hygiene and sleep conditioning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:40.480
and I think I've talked about five rules of&nbsp;
each in the sleep chapter of How Not to Age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.640
and I think the sleep chapter of How Not to&nbsp;
Diet also has the same information. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.240
warm baths—either foot baths or full-body&nbsp;
baths or showers before going to bed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:59.480
even just wearing warm socks at night—improve&nbsp;
sleep through an interesting mechanism which I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:04.640
talk about in How Not to Age. Check it out—go&nbsp;
to your local public library and check it out.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:09.440
Okay, next up: “I've taken amla—amla's&nbsp;
dried Indian gooseberry powder—and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:16.040
lowered cholesterol levels by about&nbsp;
10%.” That's great! “What if there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:20.440
was one thing to lower high blood pressure?&nbsp;
What would it be?” Hibiscus tea, probably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:26.560
or ground flaxseed would be my two best bets.&nbsp;
But less than a teaspoon a day of ground black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:32.080
sesame seeds also drops systolic blood pressure&nbsp;
by about eight points. Sustaining that alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.280
could decrease your risk of stroke by&nbsp;
about a quarter. How exciting is that?

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:47.320
Okay, Phil Hampton says, “what are my thoughts on&nbsp;
echinacea?” I talk about echinacea being useless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:56.240
in terms of upper respiratory infections. I've&nbsp;
not heard of anything about group streptococcus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:05.360
and echinacea, but if it's being—I mean, the&nbsp;
thing that we thought had the greatest evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:10.000
base for it was upper respiratory infections,&nbsp;
and it turns out it doesn't even work for that.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.200
Bonnie says, “how much fat should one have&nbsp;
at each meal to get sufficient nutrition?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:20.440
This questioner, what Bonnie is asking, is talking&nbsp;
about the absorption of fat-soluble nutrients. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:26.280
if you eat a meal without any fat in it—like a&nbsp;
salad with no nuts or seeds or avocado with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:33.000
fat-free dressing—you are not going to maximally&nbsp;
absorb all the fat-soluble phytonutrients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.960
like all those carotenoid nutrients that are so&nbsp;
beneficial, like the beta carotene in greens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:43.880
etc. It does not take much; I have a video&nbsp;
about that where I give exact values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:49.960
but it was just a few—like a few&nbsp;
walnuts or a quarter of an avocado,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:56.360
if I remember correctly. But check out that&nbsp;
video. And yes, soybeans certainly—chickpeas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:06.240
are low in fat, but soybeans may have enough fat.&nbsp;
But you know, you've got to sprinkle flaxseeds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:11.400
onto your food anyway—ground flaxseeds are part&nbsp;
of my Daily Dozen—so that's another great way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:19.480
to make sure you have just enough fat to boost&nbsp;
the absorption of your fat-soluble nutrients.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:27.840
“Sarcoma with paravertebral invasion—just got&nbsp;
diagnosed with that.” That's not good. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:33.360
sarcoma is a cancer of connective tissues.&nbsp;
Oh, that's a fantastic question. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.360
what can I actually do? That is the subject&nbsp;
of what I hope is going to be my next book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:44.040
if the publisher agrees—a book on cancer. So,&nbsp;
I'll be doing daily cancer protocols for all 16&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:51.600
or so cancers that kill 5,000 Americans or more.&nbsp;
Although sarcoma may not actually make the list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:57.800
fingers crossed. So, I just don't know, but there&nbsp;
certainly are foods that can improve survival for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:03.240
different types of cancer—different foods for&nbsp;
different cancers. But sarcoma is not one I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:07.400
looked into and may not look into for the book.&nbsp;
I don't think that actually made the list. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:14.440
think the 16th was melanoma. So, cancers more&nbsp;
rare than that, killing fewer Americans, will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:21.960
not make the list, unfortunately. Already it's&nbsp;
going to be a massive book with the Daily Dozen.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:27.200
“I get all my vitamin needs except for one—I&nbsp;
only get 300 milligrams of choline versus the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:33.600
RDA of 550. I can't eat soy and don't want to&nbsp;
eat more broccoli. Should I supplement?” No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:38.000
you shouldn't supplement. Just type in "choline"&nbsp;
on NutritionFacts.org and look at its effects on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.760
organ function. Choline is an essential nutrient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:45.720
but you don't want to get too much&nbsp;
because bad bacteria can turn choline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:51.960
into trimethylamine, which is oxidized by the&nbsp;
liver and wreaks havoc in the body. But you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.360
should be able to get all the choline you need&nbsp;
from whole foods without taking supplements.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:59.800
“Are there health issues associated&nbsp;
with silicone cooking utensils?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:05.680
Not that I've seen. It's not something I've&nbsp;
looked at particularly, but I did do a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:11.320
of videos on cooking utensils and pots&nbsp;
and pans. I assume if something was bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:17.160
it would have popped up. But I have not seen&nbsp;
anything offhand. If you do a PubMed search and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:23.960
something comes up, send it my way because&nbsp;
I cook with silicone, so I'd want to know.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.560
"My books are life-changing," says Fernanda. Thank&nbsp;
you! “Is a calcium supplement from algae better?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:38.560
No, no, no, no, no. We should not be supplementing&nbsp;
for calcium. It's not particularly effective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:56.840
it's not safe, and regardless of what kind—it's&nbsp;
just a scam. And in this case, a dangerous scam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:04.840
because you're getting too much of a calcium spike&nbsp;
in your bloodstream. Check out my videos for more.

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:09.560
Fernanda's back—would I recommend statins&nbsp;
for someone whose LDL was 190? Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:22.680
Jesus. 90 is good. No, but 190 is way too&nbsp;
high, especially with a high LP(a), which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:26.840
is largely genetically determined, so you need&nbsp;
to get your LDL absolutely as low as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:34.480
certainly under 70 for primary prevention. I would&nbsp;
go on the Portfolio Diet, which is a plant-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:40.840
diet with particular foods that lower LDL&nbsp;
cholesterol. See how far you can take diet, and if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:47.520
you can't get it down to target, then absolutely&nbsp;
talk to your physician about prescribing drugs.

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:52.720
“People at Mastering Diabetes claim that dietary&nbsp;
fat—not just any dietary fat, but particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:59.760
saturated fat—causes insulin resistance. In&nbsp;
their diet, they restrict fat to 10-15%. Is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:07.420
necessary?” Well, that certainly is more typical&nbsp;
of how our species evolved—getting about 10%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.420 --> 00:10:15.680
of calories from fat. But insulin resistance is&nbsp;
really a problem with saturated fat in the context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:23.000
of excess calorie intake. But yeah, different fats&nbsp;
are kind of a mixture of various fats, and so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:32.120
for example, there's some saturated fat in nuts,&nbsp;
even though nuts have an overall benefit. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:39.760
because of all the other wonderful things in nuts.&nbsp;
But I think if you just restrict saturated fat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:49.000
which is in meat, dairy, and junk food, you&nbsp;
should be able to reverse your insulin resistance.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:55.640
“Do I need to worry about pH value when&nbsp;
making a DIY vitamin C serum?” Oh, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.640 --> 00:11:00.360
I wouldn't mix the vitamin C and niacinamide&nbsp;
together in a serum. I would do them separately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:07.320
just because... yeah, I don't know what the&nbsp;
vitamin C would be doing to the niacinamide.

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:11.880
“Fascinated by longevity research?&nbsp;
Wild blueberry”—well, wild blueberry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:15.920
you would like my new book. “What specific&nbsp;
blood markers”—oh, interesting. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:21.840
so there are all sorts of interesting measures&nbsp;
of biological aging. There are lots of companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:29.240
trying to fight it out to be the best. Probably&nbsp;
the kind of following the epigenetic clock is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:35.320
most well-validated, but I'm not sure what&nbsp;
commercial tests are available. I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:40.560
what tests are used in a laboratory setting,&nbsp;
but not sure what's available commercially.

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:45.840
“Hello,” says Niall. “I was wondering if&nbsp;
I could get your insight on artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:51.480
sweeteners, like sucralose.” I’ve got videos on&nbsp;
sucralose. Yes, there is a health concern—it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:57.880
to do with causing dysbiosis in the gut.&nbsp;
You know, sucralose manufacturers like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:02.080
brag about how their artificial sweetener&nbsp;
doesn't get absorbed into the bloodstream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:05.000
so therefore it's safe. But guess what? If&nbsp;
it doesn't get absorbed into the bloodstream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:09.080
what does it do? It ends up in the colon,&nbsp;
where it mucks with our microbiome. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:13.400
don't do it. If you want to see the science,&nbsp;
just type in "sucralose" on NutritionFacts.org.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:21.600
“Are puffed grains unhealthy?” Yeah, puffing,&nbsp;
as well as kind of explosion-extrusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:25.720
and all sorts of things to make breakfast&nbsp;
cereals—it's just ways to kind of increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:32.080
the glycemic index, which is not something&nbsp;
we want. So yeah, I would avoid that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:37.680
even if they're whole grains. There are better&nbsp;
ways to get your grains, like whole intact grains.

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:40.720
Victoria says her husband, 63, has been&nbsp;
following a whole-food, plant-based diet for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:47.640
six years, but his total cholesterol is still&nbsp;
high. Oh my God, LDL is way too high. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:52.040
so, you know, a lot of people claim they're on&nbsp;
a whole-food, plant-based diet, but are eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:58.600
vegan junk food that has tropical oils like&nbsp;
coconut oil or something. That'd be the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:03.240
thing to check. But if you really are eating whole&nbsp;
foods and your LDL is that extraordinarily high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:07.480
I would try the Portfolio Diet, as I talked&nbsp;
about before. David Jenkins at the University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:12.520
of Toronto—that's the best diet for maximally&nbsp;
lowering cholesterol. But if you can't get it down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:18.840
to target, then you need to figure out another&nbsp;
way. There are lots of things you can take that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:24.720
also lower cholesterol, like amla and things like&nbsp;
that. But again, if you can't do it by diet, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:31.040
have to get it down some way, and that can include&nbsp;
drugs—higher-dose drugs, multiple drugs, whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:36.920
it takes to get your LDL down, which is a primary&nbsp;
risk factor for the number one killer in women.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:41.280
Wealthy says, “what do we think about&nbsp;
Peter Rogers?” Never heard of him. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:46.040
more radical version of me? How funny. Now&nbsp;
I want to Google him—never heard of him.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:51.320
“Can I roast nuts on low temperature&nbsp;
to avoid forming acrylamides?” It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:55.880
the acrylamides we worry about in nuts.&nbsp;
Acrylamides are more of a concern with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.880 --> 00:14:01.040
high-heat cooking of carbohydrates like&nbsp;
crackers, French fries, and potato chips.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:08.400
The nut roasting causes the formation&nbsp;
of advanced glycation end-products. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:14.200
think it starts occurring at like 250°F,&nbsp;
so it's not really the roasting of nuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:22.120
but if you can keep your nuts under that&nbsp;
temperature, you'll likely keep your AGEs low.

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:26.960
I find them easier to chew than raw. Raw—I&nbsp;
don't know why raw nuts would be hard to chew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:29.040
like raw walnuts—are they hard to chew?

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:35.480
“Can I eat too much protein from beans? I hear&nbsp;
doctors say more than a cup a day of beans”—well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:37.120
why can't you eat more than a cup a day? In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:41.880
I recommend in my Daily Dozen a cup and a&nbsp;
half—right, three servings a day of legumes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:45.880
with a serving being a half-cup. How many&nbsp;
beans can we eat? Why would you be hurting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:51.760
your kidneys? Beans do not hurt your kidneys.&nbsp;
In fact, the recommendation is to switch over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:59.000
to plant protein for protecting chronic kidney&nbsp;
failure patients—to protect one's kidneys by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:04.080
reducing animal protein intake. If you have&nbsp;
extremely poor kidney function, then they may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:09.400
want to decrease your protein overall, but you&nbsp;
need to talk to a renal dietitian, definitely.

00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:13.280
Anthony ate a whole-food, plant-based&nbsp;
diet for four months. Oh my God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:22.640
lost 75 pounds—congratulations! But then, in four&nbsp;
months, gained back 40 pounds. So, I talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:28.320
what is behind the stalling of weight loss and&nbsp;
gaining back weight in How Not to Diet. Go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:33.520
your local public library, get How Not to Diet,&nbsp;
and go to that chapter. Sorry to hear that.

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:40.920
Isabelle says she's a huge fan—that's nice. Can&nbsp;
I advise what a type 1 diabetic should eat to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:49.360
prevent diabetic bone disease? I don't think of&nbsp;
diabetes causing bone disease, but I mean, type 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:55.760
diabetes can be certainly serious. You know, the&nbsp;
Defeating Diabetes or Mastering Diabetes folks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.280 --> 00:16:01.280
know a lot about type 1 diabetes, so basically&nbsp;
you're trying to improve insulin resistance just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:06.480
to make your body more sensitive to the insulin&nbsp;
that you are injecting. But you will be injecting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:11.280
or your child will be injecting, insulin&nbsp;
for their whole life—critically important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:21.840
to maintain... yeah, primarily to not bottom out&nbsp;
and have life-threatening hypoglycemia. So yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:29.040
it's very important to work very closely with your&nbsp;
medical professionals to make sure that they keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:35.200
their blood sugar levels stable. We can do&nbsp;
that by improving insulin sensitivity so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:40.960
you can get into a regular regimen of knowing&nbsp;
exactly how much insulin you need every day.

00:16:40.960 --> 00:16:46.120
“How long to microwave 1.5 ounces of mushrooms&nbsp;
